const SH_2=[
// ===== alex =====
{t:"alex",lv:4,fmt:"multi",q:"Alexander's contingency framework files 'institutional design' in cell (b): real, collective, accomplishment-oriented action, next to bargaining and strategic planning. Moroni (2010) attacks Alexander's notion of institutional design. Which statement correctly combines the two readings?",o:["Moroni accepts Alexander's three levels but moves design to cell (d), since only an ideal, individual optimiser can redesign rules for one given goal.","Moroni agrees that institutional design is strategic bargaining, and objects only that bargaining yields sub-optimal rules where evolution yields optimal ones.","Moroni's quarrel is with the object, not the cell: since institutions differ from organisations, design fits organisations, not evolved rules of the game.","Moroni rejects the framework as merely descriptive and holds that institutional design must instead meet the ideal, consensus-oriented standard of cell (f)."],a:2,e:"Moroni argues there is 'a breach along the line' between Alexander's meso and macro levels because institutions (rules of the game) differ from organisations (players), so only 'organizational design' is possible and institutions call for 'institutional intervention'; where Alexander files design is not his concern. The second option is tempting because Moroni does discuss the quality of evolved rules, but it fails twice: he calls evolution sub-optimal, not optimal, and he never frames design as bargaining."},
{t:"alex",lv:4,fmt:"multi",q:"The course synthesis says Scott shows what happens when one of Alexander's cells is imposed without the others. Read through Alexander's framework, which diagnosis of Brasília as Scott describes it is correct?",o:["A formal logic of one designing authority optimising a few specified values (cell d) was imposed without the real, interactive domain where residents bargain and resist.","Planners relied on Habermas's ideal communicative action to build consensus, and because such consensus is rare in reality, the plan collapsed into unplanned settlements.","Planners satisficed in the real, individual cell instead of optimising, so Brasília lacked the comprehensive rationality that Scott says a capital city requires.","Scott and Alexander agree that Brasília calls for the 'abandonment' response: replacing rational process models with planners' own intuition and experience."],a:0,e:"Scott's high modernism is top-down optimisation of a few sharply specified values by 'the Plan', which is Alexander's ideal, individual, accomplishment-oriented cell (d); it failed because it ignored the strategic and political interaction of residents and squatters (the real, collective cells). The second option fails because Le Corbusier's plan explicitly ignored consultation and the electorate: nothing in Brasília was consensus-seeking communicative action."},
{t:"alex",lv:4,fmt:"assertion",q:"Statement I: Alexander keeps Habermas's communicative action as a filled cell of his framework, even though he notes that most observed interaction is strategic rather than communicative. Statement II: Alexander's framework separates ideal (normative) theory from real (descriptive) theory, so a model can hold as a norm where it rarely describes behaviour.",o:["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],a:0,e:"Both are true, and II gives the reason for I: communicative action sits in the ideal, interactive, consensus-oriented cell (f), so its rarity in practice does not disqualify it, just as optimisation stays valid as a norm in cell (d). 'Does not explain' is tempting, but it misses that the ideal/real split is exactly what lets Alexander keep a model he finds descriptively unrealistic."},
{t:"alex",lv:4,fmt:"mechanism",q:"Which causal chain correctly reconstructs why Alexander concludes that 'one paradigm cannot supplant another'?",o:["Classic rationality covers individual deliberation → TCA is a descriptive theory of how real groups interact → so TCA can replace rationality wherever several actors are involved → leaving rationality only for single decision-makers.","Classic rationality is procedural while TCA is substantive → each works at a different level of abstraction → the two cannot be compared on one scale → so planners must choose one per case by intuition.","Classic rationality ignores interaction → TCA cannot say how an actor decides what to pursue before entering discussion → each covers what the other misses → so a new paradigm must combine both.","Classic rationality ignores interaction → TCA ignores deliberation → strategic action is illegitimate because it uses power → so a new paradigm must rest on TCA alone and exclude strategic bargaining."],a:2,e:"Alexander calls the two models near mirror images: rationality covers pre-interactive deliberation but not interaction, TCA covers interaction but not deliberation, so any new paradigm must include both, which his contingency framework does. The first chain fails at its second link, since TCA is normative, not descriptive (Habermas says so himself); the second wrongly calls TCA substantive, when Alexander says it is as procedural as rationality."},
{t:"alex",lv:4,fmt:"transfer",q:"Hypothetical: a Dutch province must site six wind turbines. Its own team first ranks twenty sites with a formal multi-criteria optimisation; it then negotiates with three municipalities and an energy cooperative, each pressing its own aims, and the cooperative threatens to walk out. Which pairing of an Alexander analysis with a critique Davoudi would make is correct?",o:["Ranking: cell (d), ideal individual optimisation; talks: cell (f), communicative action. Davoudi: the ranking's evidence is never neutral, since it competes with ideology, interests and institutional habits.","Ranking: cell (d), ideal individual optimisation; talks: cell (b), strategic action. Davoudi: the ranking's evidence is never neutral, since it competes with ideology, interests and institutional habits.","Ranking: cell (d), ideal individual optimisation; talks: cell (b), strategic action. Davoudi: no central team can ever gather the dispersed knowledge needed, so siting is best left to market processes.","Ranking: cell (a), bounded satisficing; talks: cell (b), strategic action. Davoudi: better and faster evidence on tap would make the political bargaining phase with the parties unnecessary."],a:1,e:"A formal multi-criteria ranking is multi-objective optimisation (cell d), and parties pressing their own aims with exit threats is strategic action (cell b), not consensus-oriented communication. Davoudi's point is that evidence competes with Weiss's 'four I's' and cannot be separated from politics; the first option misfiles goal-seeking bargaining as communicative action, and the third gives Hayek's dispersed-knowledge argument, not Davoudi's."},
{t:"alex",lv:4,fmt:"multi",q:"Hindmoor models the protest problem as an N-person prisoners' dilemma, and the HC6 lecture proposes VvE negotiations over Airbnb. Where would Alexander file (1) a formal game-theoretic model of the protest dilemma and (2) an actual VvE meeting in which each owner bargains for his own interests?",o:["(1) Ideal, individual, accomplishment-oriented (cell d); (2) real, collective, accomplishment-oriented (cell b).","(1) Ideal, interactive, accomplishment-oriented (cell e); (2) real, collective, consensus-oriented (cell c).","(1) Ideal, interactive, accomplishment-oriented (cell e); (2) real, collective, accomplishment-oriented (cell b).","(1) Ideal, interactive, consensus-oriented (cell f); (2) real, collective, accomplishment-oriented (cell b)."],a:2,e:"Game theory and formal coalition models form Alexander's ideal, interactive, accomplishment-oriented cell (e), because outcomes depend on several actors' choices; real bargaining in which each party pursues its own goals is strategic action in cell (b). The second option is tempting because a VvE is a collective body, but cell (c) requires neutral or consensus-oriented participants, which self-interested bargaining owners are not."},
// ===== rc =====
{t:"rc",lv:4,fmt:"multi",q:"The HC6 lecture says that in rational choice theory the public interest is 'the aggregate result of the private interests'; Needham says authorities uphold 'the identifiable interests of many identifiable private persons'. Which statement about how the two relate is correct?",o:["Needham, unlike rational choice theory, keeps an objective public interest, but locates it in the shared interests of identifiable persons rather than the state.","Both hold that a planner can compute the public interest by adding up what people are willing to pay, as in Coase's noisy factory example.","Needham rejects the aggregate view because he denies that people have stable, self-interested preferences about how land is used.","Both deny an objective public interest above individuals, but Needham adds that which interests get protected, and how, is a political and technical choice."],a:3,e:"Both readings dissolve 'the public' into private persons; Needham's extra step is that the state decides which interests to protect at all (the pink door gets nothing) and whether by right or by regulation (right of support versus clean air). The first option is tempting because Needham sounds more public-minded, but he finds an objective public interest hard to substantiate and depersonalising: 'we too are the public!'"},
{t:"rc",lv:4,fmt:"multi",q:"Four institutionalists comment on Bouwmeester's Swiss hypothesis (the BGBB caps farmland prices and bars non-farmers from buying farmland). Which comment matches the logic of the strand it is attributed to?",o:["Historical institutionalist: the BGBB works because farmers have internalised it as appropriate, so they no longer even consider selling for development.","Rational choice institutionalist: the BGBB changes payoffs, so with no outsider speculation lobby, backing densification becomes insiders' best strategy.","Evolutionary institutionalist: the BGBB proves that planners can engineer property rules around a densification target, a model for reform elsewhere.","Sociological institutionalist: the BGBB is a legacy that locks in densification whatever actors choose, until a later crisis opens a critical juncture."],a:1,e:"The hypothesis is rational choice institutionalism: a rule changes incentives (no speculative gain for outsiders, scarcity rents for insiders), so the rational strategy changes. The last option is the most tempting because it sounds structural, but lock-in and critical junctures belong to historical institutionalism (and even Sorensen rejects lock-in); internalised appropriateness is sociological, and engineering rules to a target is what Moroni rejects."},
{t:"rc",lv:4,fmt:"assertion",q:"Statement I: In the lecture's noisy factory example, with clearly defined rights and zero transaction costs, the factory ends up insulated whoever holds the right. Statement II: Coase's theorem implies that the initial allocation of the right makes no difference to the parties involved.",o:["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],a:2,e:"I is true: whoever holds the right, bargaining leads to insulation, because quiet (€1,000) is worth more than insulation (€700). II is false: the allocation does not change the efficient outcome, but it decides who pays whom (the homeowner pays €700, or the factory bears the cost itself), so it matters a great deal to the parties; mistaking outcome-neutrality for distributional neutrality is the trap."},
{t:"rc",lv:4,fmt:"mechanism",q:"The lecture lists a hosting tax paid into a neighbourhood fund as a Coasean-inspired tool for Airbnb. Which causal chain explains why it counts as Coasean?",o:["Hosts impose costs on neighbours that their prices do not reflect → hundreds of neighbours cannot bargain with hundreds of hosts at low cost → a fund lets hosts compensate the neighbourhood as a group → the externality is partly priced in.","Hosts impose costs on neighbours that their prices do not reflect → because rights are clearly defined, transaction costs between neighbours and hosts are already zero → a fund simply formalises this bargain → the externality is fully priced in.","Hosts impose costs on neighbours that their prices do not reflect → neighbours form a privileged group in which one resident pays for everyone → a fund repays that resident → the externality disappears without any regulation.","Hosts impose costs on neighbours that their prices do not reflect → hundreds of neighbours cannot bargain with hundreds of hosts at low cost → a fund makes guests stop causing noise → the externality disappears without any compensation."],a:0,e:"Coasean tools clarify rights or lower transaction costs so that the party causing harm effectively compensates the harmed; a fund collects payment from hosts and returns value to the affected neighbourhood, replacing impossible one-to-one bargaining. The second chain fails because transaction costs are never zero (that is why intervention is needed), and the last wrongly assumes the tax removes the behaviour instead of pricing it."},
{t:"rc",lv:4,fmt:"transfer",q:"Hypothetical: a Dutch city downzones a historic canal district to protect its character. Twelve owners of large plots quickly hire a lawyer and claim compensation for lost value, while the thousands of residents who gain from the protection stay silent. Which pairing of a rational choice analysis with a sociological institutionalist critique is correct?",o:["RC: the owners act irrationally, since protection will raise long-run values; SI critique: owners' sense of entitlement reflects internalised norms about property, so changing payoffs alone may not change their behaviour.","RC: owners claim compensation for losses though they would keep any gain, and the few organise more easily than the many; SI critique: the result was fixed by a path set at an earlier critical juncture in planning law.","RC: owners claim compensation for losses though they would keep any gain, and the few organise more easily than the many; SI critique: owners' sense of entitlement reflects internalised property norms, not only payoffs.","RC: owners claim compensation for losses though they would keep any gain, and the few organise more easily than the many; SI critique: property rules evolved unintentionally, so planners should never have downzoned."],a:2,e:"Rational choice reads this as the betterment/compensation asymmetry plus Olson: a small group with concentrated stakes organises, a large diffuse group free-rides. A sociological institutionalist would object that claims of entitlement are shaped by internalised norms and templates of appropriateness, not only by calculation; the second option's critique is historical institutionalism and the fourth is evolutionary (Moroni)."},
{t:"rc",lv:4,fmt:"dimension",q:"Which placement of rational choice theory (HC6) on reproduction vs transformation AND universal vs situated knowledge, with its justification, matches the lecture?",o:["Reproduction only, since self-interested insiders always defend existing rights; universal, since it assumes the same rational behaviour everywhere and seeks general models.","Both reproduction and transformation; situated, since incentives depend on local property law, as the contrast between Swiss and Dutch rules shows.","Transformation only, since rules can always be redesigned at will; universal, since it assumes the same rational behaviour everywhere and seeks general models.","Both: insiders protect interests and actors can redesign rules; universal, since it assumes the same rational behaviour everywhere and seeks general models."],a:3,e:"The lecture says rational choice can explain both reproduction (insiders using strategic positions) and transformation (deliberate redesign of rules and incentives), and that its knowledge stance is positivist, assuming universal rational behaviour and aiming at generalisable explanations. The second option is tempting because the Swiss hypothesis depends on a local law, but the theory applies one universal model of behaviour to different rule settings: the variation lies in the rules, not in the knowledge claim."},
{t:"rc",lv:4,fmt:"assertion",q:"Statement I: The HC6 lecture calls rational choice an agency-focused theory. Statement II: The HC6 lecture holds that property rights and other institutions shape actors' incentives and therefore their strategies.",o:["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],a:1,e:"Both are true, but II does not explain I; if anything it pulls the other way, since institutions shaping incentives is a structural element. The theory is agency-focused because it explains outcomes from individuals maximising self-interest, with rules as the setting of those choices; reading II as the reason for I confuses the context of choice with the source of explanation."},
// ===== needham =====
{t:"needham",lv:4,fmt:"multi",q:"The HC6 lecture presents Coase's theorem as a route to the efficient outcome. Needham also draws on Coase. Which statement correctly combines the two?",o:["Needham takes Coase's reciprocity of harm but judges rights and regulation by how well they reach politically set goals, not by assumed efficiency.","Needham takes Coase's efficiency criterion, and adds that land markets come close to zero transaction costs once all rights are clearly registered.","Needham rejects Coase entirely, since for Needham property rights are natural and so cannot be reallocated to whoever values them most.","Needham uses Coase to show that the initial allocation of rights is irrelevant to land use, so planning goals can simply be left to private bargaining."],a:0,e:"Needham borrows Coase's point that restraining A to protect B harms A ('Should A be allowed to harm B or should B be allowed to harm A?') but evaluates instruments with Williamson's 'remediability', comparing effectiveness for given political goals instead of assuming policy aims at maximum efficiency. The last option is tempting because it sounds Coasean, but Needham keeps the planning goals, finds markets in rights feasible only in limited circumstances, and his clean-air case shows transaction costs are far from zero."},
{t:"needham",lv:4,fmt:"multi",q:"Needham calls property rights a 'policy variable'; Moroni wants reformers to 'recognize' rather than 'invent'. Which statement about where each thinks property rules come from, and what follows for reform, is correct?",o:["Needham follows Locke in seeing rights as natural and therefore hard to change; Moroni sees them as designed by states, so they are open to quick redesign.","Both see rights as created by state decision, but Moroni adds that such decisions should be left to judges and courts rather than to planners or politicians.","Needham sees rights as emergent conventions like language; Moroni sees them as state creations that planners may redesign at will to reach goals.","Needham: rights exist because the state protects them, so can be redesigned; Moroni: property rules grew from experience as conventions, so reform is gradual."],a:3,e:"Needham quotes Bromley ('it is the fact that something is protected that makes it a right') and concludes rights can be deliberately changed; Moroni follows Hume, for whom stable possession arose gradually from repeated experience, like language, hence modesty and gradualism. The third option is the tempting mirror image: it swaps the two authors, and Moroni never says planners may redesign institutions at will."},
{t:"needham",lv:4,fmt:"assertion",q:"Statement I: Needham notes that in the Netherlands the amenity of a housing estate cannot at present be secured by covenants enforced by a residents' association. Statement II: Needham argues that covenants fail in principle for housing estates, because the effects of neighbours' actions are too dispersed for private-law protection.",o:["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],a:2,e:"I is true: Dutch property law cannot oblige owners to join such an association, so the US-style solution is not binding there. II is false: dispersed effects are Needham's reason why markets in rights hardly work for coherent regional development, not for housing estates, where he finds covenants feasible; the Dutch barrier is legal, not technical."},
{t:"needham",lv:4,fmt:"mechanism",q:"Which causal chain correctly reconstructs how Needham gets from the nature of property rights to 'creating markets in property rights'?",o:["Rights are natural and prior to the state (Locke) → the state can only recognise them → the split between rights and regulation is fixed → so planning goals must be pursued by regulation alone.","A right exists because it is protected (Bromley) → so the split between private-law and public-law protection is a decision → it can be revised → moving protection from public to private law creates markets in rights.","A right exists because it is protected (Bromley) → so the split between private-law and public-law protection is a decision → it can be revised → so markets in rights should replace land-use planning wherever possible.","A right exists because it is protected (Bromley) → so all rights are efficient by definition → any revision lowers welfare → so markets in rights arise only where planning has already failed."],a:1,e:"Because rights are created and protected by the state, the line between interests protected as rights and interests protected by planning is a political decision that can be redrawn; shifting protection to private law is what Needham means by creating markets in property rights. The third chain is tempting but breaks at its last link: the chapter 'does not recommend replacing land-use planning by markets' and finds them feasible only under limited circumstances."},
{t:"needham",lv:4,fmt:"transfer",q:"Hypothetical: to save on upkeep, a Dutch city plans to make the residents of a new upmarket neighbourhood co-owners of its playground and green strip, bound by covenants, with public access guaranteed by a planning condition. Which pairing of a Needham analysis with a critique a Marxist (HC4) would make is correct?",o:["Needham: protection shifts partly from public to private law, a privately owned public space feasible only in some conditions; Marxist: rights in land entrench owners' class position, so owner-run 'commons' reproduce exclusion.","Needham: protection shifts partly from public to private law, a privately owned public space feasible only in some conditions; Marxist: the co-owners will free-ride on each other's upkeep, so the shared space will be under-provided.","Needham: this is purely public-law protection, since the city sets a planning condition and keeps every right itself; Marxist: rights in land entrench owners' class position, so owner-run 'commons' reproduce exclusion.","Needham: protection shifts partly from public to private law, a privately owned public space feasible only in some conditions; Marxist: evolved property rules should be recognised, not redesigned by a city for one budget goal."],a:0,e:"Needham lists privately owned, publicly accessible spaces under planning conditions as rights deliberately created for a type of urban environment, and warns of exclusive developments and fragmentation. A Marxist critique targets private property as the basis of class division; the second option's critique is Olson's free-riding and the fourth is Moroni's, while the third misreads the case, since the residents' new rights are private-law protection."},
{t:"needham",lv:4,fmt:"dimension",q:"Which placement of Needham (2012) on reproduction vs transformation AND deliberate vs emergent change, with its justification, matches the course reading?",o:["Reproduction and deliberate: rights carry high ideological content and the ECHR limits change, so the state can at most protect the existing allocation.","Transformation and emergent: once markets in rights exist, land use evolves through voluntary exchange that no authority designs or steers.","Transformation and deliberate: since rights are made by the state, they 'can be deliberately changed in order purposefully to influence land use'.","Reproduction and emergent: rights reflect long-evolved conventions, so changing who holds them lies beyond the reach of public authorities."],a:2,e:"The lecture uses Needham's 'property rights can be deliberately changed' line as its example of rational choice explaining transformation through deliberate redesign of rules. The first option is tempting because Needham does say rights change slowly given their ideological content and the ECHR, but that is a brake on change, not a denial of it, and the whole chapter explores deliberate revision."},
// ===== olson =====
{t:"olson",lv:4,fmt:"multi",q:"Taylor shows that Melbourne's landowners ('insiders') beat future homebuyers ('outsiders') over the GAIC. Which combination of Olson's logic, as presented by Hindmoor, with Taylor's case is correct?",o:["Landowners formed a privileged group in which one owner paid for the whole campaign; buyers were an intermediate group able to monitor each other's contributions.","Each owner had a large, concentrated stake, so owners organised; future buyers are a diffuse, latent group, many not yet identifiable, so none bore the cost.","Landowners organised only because campaigning gave them process benefits; buyers faced an assurance game, so their cooperation should have been easy to achieve.","Both groups stayed latent, so the outcome was decided by housing developers, who led the opposition and pushed the charge onto future purchasers."],a:1,e:"Concentrated, high stakes made organising worthwhile for fringe owners (70 percent of the sampled submissions; the Taxed Out! campaign), while future buyers pay later, each a little, and partly do not yet exist as a group: Taylor's insider-outsider problem read through Olson. The last option fails on the facts: Taylor found opposition was driven by landowners rather than developers, who accept transparent charges they can pass on."},
{t:"olson",lv:4,fmt:"multi",q:"Hindmoor cites revolution as a collective action problem: workers would gain from it but let others risk the barricades. Which statement correctly relates the course's Marx to Olson?",o:["Marx expects shared class position to drive class struggle; Olson warns a shared interest does not by itself yield collective action, though leaders can help.","Olson confirms Marx: once workers understand their shared class interest, each gains from joining, so free-riding disappears as class consciousness grows.","Olson refutes Marx: free-riding makes revolutions impossible, which is why Hindmoor treats leaders such as Trotsky or Castro as exceptions to the theory.","Marx and Olson agree on method: both explain group outcomes mainly from structural positions in the economy rather than from individual cost-benefit choices."],a:0,e:"For Marx, class struggle over the means of production drives history; Olson's point is that each worker reasons that his own contribution hardly matters, so a common interest is not enough, while Hindmoor adds that entrepreneurs such as Trotsky or Castro can break the second-order problem. The third option is the misreading Hindmoor rejects: Olson shows a problem, not impossibility, and entrepreneurs are part of the account, not exceptions."},
{t:"olson",lv:4,fmt:"assertion",q:"Statement I: In Hindmoor's assurance game (the dam holds only if both farmers work), collective action should be easily achieved. Statement II: In the assurance game, cooperating is each farmer's dominant strategy.",o:["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],a:2,e:"I is true: both farmers prefer mutual cooperation and know it, so cooperation should be easy. II is false: the assurance game has no dominant strategy, since each farmer's best move depends on the other (working alone is the worst outcome); cooperation is easy because of shared preferences and common knowledge, not dominance, which is what separates it from the prisoners' dilemma, where defection is dominant."},
{t:"olson",lv:4,fmt:"mechanism",q:"Which causal chain correctly reconstructs why members of Israeli kibbutzim work hard, in Hindmoor's account?",o:["Everyone receives housing and food regardless of effort → shirking pays individually → hard workers are rewarded with extra housing, a positive selective incentive → so the collective good is provided.","Everyone receives housing and food regardless of effort → shirking pays individually → shirkers face gossip, but gossiping is costly to the gossiper → so a second-order problem remains unsolved.","Everyone receives housing and food regardless of effort → shirking pays individually → shirkers face vicious gossip → gossiping is enjoyable in itself, so no second-order problem arises.","Everyone receives housing and food in proportion to effort → shirking does not pay individually → gossip merely reflects existing effort levels → so there is no collective action problem to solve."],a:2,e:"Equal rewards create a free-riding incentive; gossip punishes shirkers, and because gossiping is a pleasure (a process benefit) nobody needs an incentive to apply the sanction, so the second-order problem does not arise; costly exit helps too. The second chain is tempting because sanctioning usually is a second-order collective good, but Hindmoor's point is exactly that enjoyable gossip escapes it."},
{t:"olson",lv:4,fmt:"transfer",q:"Hypothetical: 300 households on a Dutch estate would all gain from a car-free courtyard, which needs a joint petition and a small yearly fee; only 20 join. The residents' committee then gives members first claim on the new bike shed, and membership rises. Which pairing of an Olson analysis with a sociological institutionalist critique is correct?",o:["Olson: the estate is a privileged group, since one keen resident will pay for everyone; SI critique: residents' preferences are formed by internalised norms of what a good neighbour does, not just by payoffs.","Olson: a latent group free-rides until a selective incentive (bike shed priority) rewards members; SI critique: participation stays low because of a path set at an earlier critical juncture.","Olson: a latent group free-rides until a selective incentive (bike shed priority) rewards members; SI critique: rules like this should evolve by trial and error rather than be designed by a committee.","Olson: a latent group free-rides until a selective incentive (bike shed priority) rewards members; SI critique: preferences are formed by internalised norms of what a good neighbour does, not just by payoffs."],a:3,e:"The courtyard is non-excludable, so each household free-rides; bike shed priority goes only to members, a positive selective incentive. A sociological institutionalist would say institutions 'establish the very criteria by which people discover their preferences', so norms, not only incentives, drive joining; the second critique is historical institutionalism, the third evolutionary, and the first option misuses 'privileged group', which needs a member willing to bear the whole cost."},
{t:"olson",lv:4,fmt:"dimension",q:"Which placement of Olson's logic of collective action (Hindmoor ch. 5) on structure vs agency AND universal vs situated knowledge, with its justification, is correct?",o:["Structure: groups only succeed when the state coerces or institutions set rules; universal: one model of self-interest applies to unions, protests and states.","Agency: collective outcomes follow from individuals weighing costs and benefits; universal: one model of self-interest applies to unions, protests and states.","Agency: collective outcomes follow from individuals weighing costs and benefits; situated: Hindmoor stresses that group size predicts latency only as a tendency.","Structure: free-riding is built into the nature of public goods themselves; situated: each collective good needs its own theory of why groups form."],a:1,e:"Olson explains collective outcomes from individual, self-interested choices (methodological individualism) and applies the same rationality assumption to unions, revolutions, emissions and security: a general, model-based claim. The third option is tempting because Hindmoor calls the size effect 'at most, an empirical tendency', but that qualifies one prediction; it does not make the theory's knowledge claim situated."},
// ===== taylor =====
{t:"taylor",lv:4,fmt:"multi",q:"Taylor (Melbourne) and Sorensen (Toronto) both study contests over capturing land value uplift. Which statement correctly combines the two?",o:["Taylor treats the landowners' campaign as a critical juncture that locked in 'purchaser pays', while Sorensen explains Toronto's cuts by homevoter rent-seeking.","Both find that housing developers led the opposition to value capture, while landowners and municipalities mostly accepted the charges as transparent.","Both read value capture as path-dependent lock-in, so neither expects the rules on who pays for growth to change once they have been set.","Taylor explains one episode by insiders' rent-seeking; Sorensen explains decades of change by junctures and bounded pathways; both see who pays as contested."],a:3,e:"Taylor applies rational choice (rent-seeking, insiders versus outsiders) to a single episode, from the 2008 proposal to the 2011 deferral; Sorensen applies historical institutionalism, with critical junctures, reactive sequences and a developmental pathway, from 1946 to 2023. The second option is tempting because Toronto's developers did lobby hard, but Taylor explicitly found that opposition to the GAIC was driven by landowners rather than housing developers."},
{t:"taylor",lv:4,fmt:"multi",q:"The Growth Areas Authority cited research that rural land worth $15,000 to $35,000 per hectare rose to about $365,000 once inside the UGB and rezoned, yet landowners discredited the expected windfall and the GAIC was weakened. How does this relate to Davoudi's critique of evidence-based policy?",o:["It contradicts Davoudi: the evidence came from positive science, so on her account it should have prevailed over the owners' unsupported claims.","It illustrates Davoudi's enlightenment model: research slowly reshaped landowners' understanding, so the charge was redesigned to match the evidence.","It supports the instrumental model Davoudi defends: better and faster evidence on tap would have let the government keep the original charge.","It fits Davoudi: evidence does not flow linearly into policy but competes with interests and ideology, here owners' self-interest taken as equitable."],a:3,e:"Davoudi argues that evidence competes with Weiss's 'four I's' (ideology, interests, institutions, information) and that the linear, instrumental model is naive; Taylor shows interests beating the betterment evidence, with self-interest 'taken at face value as more equitable'. The first option gets Davoudi backwards: she criticises the assumption that positivist evidence will or should prevail, and the third misstates her, since she criticises the instrumental model."},
{t:"taylor",lv:4,fmt:"assertion",q:"Statement I: In Taylor's account, the 2011 decision to defer the GAIC fully until development was presented as protecting hobby farmers and lifestyle owners. Statement II: Under full deferral, owners who keep their land in rural use pay nothing unless and until that land is developed.",o:["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],a:0,e:"Both are true, and II explains I: lifestyle and hobby-farm owners ('homevoters') did not plan to develop, so a charge due only at development spares them, whereas the original design was payable at sale or subdivision. 'Does not explain' is tempting if II is read as a mere technicality, but this timing is exactly why the deferral could be presented as protecting them."},
{t:"taylor",lv:4,fmt:"mechanism",q:"Which causal chain correctly reconstructs how the GAIC came to be weakened, according to Taylor?",o:["The GAIC fell due at development only → owners accepted the windfall logic but wanted the money for local roads → developers lobbied against any charge → so the bill was redrafted to exempt developers.","The GAIC fell due at sale or subdivision, so ordinary vendors could be hit → owners framed it as a tax on existing value, not a windfall → the framing was taken as equitable → so the bill was redrafted with exemptions and buyer liability.","The GAIC fell due at sale or subdivision, so ordinary vendors could be hit → owners framed it as a tax on existing value, not a windfall → the opposition blocked it because it opposed any UGB expansion → so the bill was dropped altogether.","The GAIC fell due at sale or subdivision, so ordinary vendors could be hit → owners framed it as a tax on existing value, not a windfall → new research then showed no betterment had occurred → so the charge was cut to fit the evidence."],a:1,e:"Payability at sale exposed ordinary vendors, who discredited betterment and recast the charge as a tax on existing equity; this representation of self-interest gained traction, and the bill was redrafted (small-parcel exemptions, liability shifted to purchasers) and later fully deferred. The third chain fails at its third link: the opposition supported the expansion but not the charge, and the GAIC was modified, not dropped; the fourth contradicts the press reports of huge windfalls."},
{t:"taylor",lv:4,fmt:"transfer",q:"Hypothetical: a rural Dutch municipality rezones farmland for a business park and plans a levy on the value gain, payable when the land is sold. Farmers argue no gain will ever materialise, and those with no plans to sell protest loudest. Which pairing of a Taylor-style analysis with a historical institutionalist critique is correct?",o:["Taylor: farmers rationally welcome the levy, since it funds infrastructure that raises their land value; HI critique: the outcome depends on a legal pathway set at earlier critical junctures, not on one round of lobbying.","Taylor: insiders discredit the betterment to avoid the levy and push it onto later buyers; HI critique: the farmers' claims reflect internalised norms of appropriateness, not strategic calculation.","Taylor: insiders discredit the betterment to avoid the levy and push it onto later buyers; HI critique: the outcome depends on a legal pathway set at earlier critical junctures, not on one round of lobbying.","Taylor: insiders discredit the betterment to avoid the levy and push it onto later buyers; HI critique: evolved property rules embody dispersed knowledge, so no new levy should be designed."],a:2,e:"As in Melbourne, owners without development plans frame the levy as a tax on existing value and try to shift it to purchasers: rent-seeking by insiders. A historical institutionalist such as Sorensen would object that one episode of lobbying is read without the longer sequence of junctures and pathways that decides which value capture tools exist and how contested they are; the second critique is sociological and the fourth evolutionary."},
{t:"taylor",lv:4,fmt:"dimension",q:"Which placement of Taylor (2016) on structure vs agency AND reproduction vs transformation, with its justification, matches the course reading?",o:["Agency and transformation: the landowners' lobbying actually changed the rules, so the GAIC bill was redrafted and then deferred.","Structure and reproduction: the UGB itself creates the rent, so landowners' behaviour simply follows from where the line is drawn.","Structure and transformation: the boundary shift moved land values, so the property market was restructured whatever owners did.","Agency and reproduction: strategic owners lobbied within the boundary regime, kept their advantage and moved costs to outsiders."],a:3,e:"Taylor explains outcomes by owners' strategic rent-seeking (agency within the rules that create the rent), and the result reproduces existing owners' advantage, passing costs to future buyers. The first option is the trap: the rules did change, but the change protected insiders' position, so the outcome is reproduction rather than transformation."},
// ===== ni =====
{t:"ni",lv:4,fmt:"multi",q:"Ostrom appears in Hindmoor's Olson chapter, in the HC7 lecture and in Dembski & Salet's Table 1. Which statement correctly reports how each places her?",o:["Hindmoor: coercion by a leviathan; HC7: historical institutionalism; Dembski & Salet: internalised and validated in action, next to sociological institutionalism.","Hindmoor: conditional cooperation without leviathan or market; HC7: rational choice institutionalism; Dembski & Salet: actor-centred, external but in action.","Hindmoor: conditional cooperation without leviathan or market; HC7: sociological institutionalism; Dembski & Salet: rational choice, external and prior to action.","Hindmoor: privatisation and tradeable permits after Coase; HC7: rational choice institutionalism; Dembski & Salet: actor-centred, internalised and prior to action."],a:1,e:"Hindmoor uses Ostrom for conditional cooperation: communities craft rules of allocation, monitoring and sanction without a leviathan or the market; the lecture lists her commons work under rational choice institutionalism; Dembski & Salet place Ostrom and Scharpf in political or actor-centred institutionalism, external but 'in action'. The third option is tempting for its Table 1 part, since the lecture does call her rational choice, but Table 1 separates her from the rational choice quadrant on the in-action dimension."},
{t:"ni",lv:4,fmt:"multi",q:"Hypothetical: Amsterdam wants to cut car use in its centre. Each option gives one strand's diagnosis and the planner's role that follows from it. Which option is consistent with the HC7 map on both counts?",o:["Sociological: driving is internalised as appropriate behaviour; planners can therefore redefine its meaning by decree through a strong campaign slogan.","Historical: car dependence is a path set by 1960s choices; planners can therefore change nothing until that lock-in breaks down by itself.","Rational choice: drivers respond to the payoffs set by parking rules; planners therefore act as rule designers who change prices, permits and quotas.","Evolutionary: traffic rules evolved and store dispersed knowledge; planners should therefore refrain from any intervention in those rules."],a:2,e:"On the map, rational choice institutionalism treats institutions as rules that set payoffs and sees planners as rule designers (Ostrom, Coase). Every other diagnosis is right, but its planner role carries the strand's classic trap: symbols 'cannot be used in a voluntaristic way' (Dembski & Salet), path dependence is usually 'not a matter of lock-in' (Sorensen), and evolution 'does not prevent us from intervening' (Moroni)."},
{t:"ni",lv:4,fmt:"assertion",q:"Statement I: Because institutions are the context of planning, new institutionalism holds that planners cannot change them. Statement II: The HC7 lecture describes a 'double life' of institutions: they constrain actors but are also outcomes of human action.",o:["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],a:3,e:"I is false and II is true. Savini's line is that institutions are 'both the context and object of planning': they constrain, but planners also produce new rules and arrangements, which is exactly the double life in II. The strands disagree on how much deliberate change is possible, not on whether institutions can change at all, so II in fact refutes I."},
{t:"ni",lv:4,fmt:"mechanism",q:"Which causal chain correctly describes how sociological institutionalism, on the HC7 map, explains why one planning arrangement spreads across many municipalities?",o:["The arrangement fits established templates seen as legitimate → actors adopt it as appropriate, not because it pays → it is reproduced from one municipality to the next → so practices converge even without clear gains.","The arrangement fits established templates seen as legitimate → actors adopt it because it maximises their own payoffs → it is copied wherever payoffs are similar → so practices converge only where they bring clear gains.","The arrangement fits established templates seen as legitimate → a crisis then opens a critical juncture → the choice made at that moment is locked in → so practices converge because nobody can contest them afterwards.","The arrangement fits established templates seen as legitimate → variants compete in unplanned trial and error → the most efficient variant is selected → so practices converge on the best solution available."],a:0,e:"Goodness-of-fit (DiMaggio and Powell) means arrangements are reproduced because they fit templates seen as legitimate; actors follow a logic of appropriateness, so convergence need not track efficiency. The second chain is tempting because it starts correctly, but its second link swaps in rational choice payoffs; the third imports historical lock-in and the fourth an evolutionary selection story."},
{t:"ni",lv:4,fmt:"transfer",q:"Hypothetical: Utrecht requires every large housing project to set up a 'neighbourhood table' where residents co-decide on public space. Five years on, most tables exist on paper but rarely meet. Which pairing of a rational choice institutionalist analysis with a sociological institutionalist critique is correct?",o:["RC: the tables fail because the rule does not fit residents' templates of legitimate participation; SI critique: meeting is costly and its benefits diffuse, so residents simply free-ride on the few who attend.","RC: meeting is costly and benefits are diffuse, so residents free-ride unless the rule changes payoffs; SI critique: a rule that misfits internalised scripts of appropriate conduct stays empty, whatever the payoffs.","RC: meeting is costly and benefits are diffuse, so residents free-ride unless the rule changes payoffs; SI critique: participation stays weak because of a path set at an earlier critical juncture in Dutch planning law.","RC: meeting is costly and benefits are diffuse, so residents free-ride unless the rule changes payoffs; SI critique: such rules should evolve through trial and error rather than be imposed by a city."],a:1,e:"Rational choice institutionalism sees rules as external constraints working through payoffs, so a costly, low-benefit duty yields free-riding. The sociological critique is that institutions only work when internalised as appropriate (logic of appropriateness, goodness-of-fit); the third critique is Holsen-style historical institutionalism and the fourth Moroni's, while the first option swaps the two strands."},
{t:"ni",lv:4,fmt:"dimension",q:"Which placement of one strand on structure vs agency AND deliberate vs emergent change matches the HC7 map and its course synthesis?",o:["Sociological: internalised structure that is re-enacted in practice; change purely emergent, beyond any deliberate steering by planners.","Rational choice: agency within the rules; change emerges through unplanned trial-and-error selection of the most efficient rules.","Evolutionary: emergent change by trial and error; structure so dominant that actors' intentional actions play no role at all in it.","Historical: structure-leaning, with moments of agency at critical junctures; change through junctures followed by bounded pathways."],a:3,e:"The synthesis places historical institutionalism toward structure, with agency concentrated in critical junctures, and the map gives its change mechanism as critical junctures followed by bounded pathways. The first option is tempting because its first half is right, but the map says sociological change is 'deliberate but must be embedded'; the third fails because for Moroni variation comes from intentional agents' trial and error."},
{t:"ni",lv:4,fmt:"assertion",q:"Statement I: In Dembski & Salet's Table 1, historical institutionalism treats institutions as internalised rather than as external constraints. Statement II: On the HC7 map, historical institutionalism stresses path dependency and structural elements.",o:["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],a:1,e:"Both are true, but II does not explain I. Path dependency speaks to Table 1's other dimension (institutions fixed 'prior to action' rather than validated in action), whereas internalisation concerns where institutions sit: in actors' mindsets rather than outside them. Linking the two is the trap: rational choice institutionalism is also 'prior to action' yet external."},
// ===== dembski =====
{t:"dembski",lv:4,fmt:"multi",q:"Scott describes Brasília as meant to transform how Brazilians lived, while most residents ended up in unplanned settlements. Which reading combining Scott with Dembski & Salet is correct?",o:["Dembski & Salet would blame the design: the capital failed as a symbol because its architecture was too monotonous, so a better design would have worked.","For D&S the capital was a symbol used voluntaristically, its meaning never inculcated in residents' habitus; Scott adds that order rests on practical knowledge.","Dembski & Salet would read the unplanned settlements as proof that institutions are 'rusty old fixtures', so transformation through symbols is impossible in principle.","Scott and Dembski & Salet agree that new social meaning should be engineered from the top, provided that residents are consulted before the plan is drawn."],a:1,e:"Dembski & Salet hold that success does not lie in the quality of the symbol but in its embedding in the audience's mental maps, and that symbols 'cannot be used in a voluntaristic way'; Brasília's new way of life was decreed from above, while Scott, via Jacobs, stresses everyday practical knowledge. The first option is tempting because 'brasilite' was real, but redesigning the symbol is exactly the reflex the authors say misses the point."},
{t:"dembski",lv:4,fmt:"assertion",q:"Statement I: For Dembski & Salet, the success or failure of a symbolic marker lies in its institutional embedding rather than in the quality of the symbol. Statement II: For Dembski & Salet, a rite of institution works its 'social magic' only if its meaning is inculcated in, and guaranteed by, its audience.",o:["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],a:0,e:"Both are true, and II explains I: because a symbol's power depends on the audience recognising and guaranteeing its meaning (the 'Achilles heel' of rites of institution), what decides success is embedding in mental maps, not design quality. 'Does not explain' is tempting because II concerns rites and I planning symbols, but the authors use the rite mechanism precisely to explain why planning symbols succeed or fail."},
{t:"dembski",lv:4,fmt:"mechanism",q:"Which causal chain correctly reconstructs why, for Dembski & Salet, a symbol of transformation has a harder job than a preservation concept such as the Green Heart?",o:["A transformation symbol proposes a meaning not yet held → institutions are fixed prior to action, so norms cannot shift → old perceptions persist indefinitely → so only preservation symbols can ever succeed.","A transformation symbol proposes a meaning not yet held → its design is usually weaker than that of established concepts → audiences therefore reject it → so planners should redesign the symbol until it persuades.","A transformation symbol proposes a meaning not yet held → norms change gradually, since few reinterpret at once → many cling to old perceptions → so it works best where power struggles over the norm are at their most intense.","A transformation symbol proposes a meaning not yet held → norms change gradually, since few reinterpret at once → many cling to old perceptions → so it works best in under-institutionalised niches where new practices take root."],a:3,e:"A preservation concept reaffirms meanings already institutionalised; a transformation symbol needs new practices to become institutionalised, and because not everyone changes interpretation at once, many cling to old perceptions; hence 'the art of planning is to find niches of underinstitutionalised space'. The third chain breaks only at the end: niches are places not dominated by power struggles over the norm, such as forgotten industrial sites."},
{t:"dembski",lv:4,fmt:"transfer",q:"Hypothetical: Rotterdam brands a former port area 'Energy Harbour'. A skate collective and small start-ups move onto a forgotten pier nobody claimed and use it daily; ten years later residents use the name as a matter of course. Which pairing of a Dembski & Salet analysis with a rational choice critique is correct?",o:["D&S: new practices took root in an under-institutionalised niche and were reaffirmed daily until the meaning was internalised; RC critique: users came because the space was cheap, so the label mattered only if it changed payoffs.","D&S: the brand succeeded because the logo and launch festival were well designed, which shows that symbol quality decides; RC critique: users came because the space was cheap, so the label mattered only if it changed payoffs.","D&S: new practices took root in an under-institutionalised niche and were reaffirmed daily until the meaning was internalised; RC critique: the meaning followed the port's industrial past along a path fixed at a critical juncture.","D&S: new practices took root in an under-institutionalised niche and were reaffirmed daily until the meaning was internalised; RC critique: the order arose spontaneously as a kosmos, so the city's branding effort was irrelevant."],a:0,e:"Dembski & Salet expect symbols to work where new cultural practices take root in niches without settled claims, and through everyday, banal reaffirmation that makes the meaning internalised. A rational choice critic would reduce the story to incentives: actors moved for cost and payoff reasons. The third critique is historical institutionalism and the fourth evolutionary, while the second option contradicts the authors' claim that success does not lie in the symbol's quality."},
{t:"dembski",lv:4,fmt:"dimension",q:"Which placement of Dembski & Salet on structure vs agency AND universal vs situated knowledge, with its justification, matches the course reading?",o:["Agency: since reality is socially constructed, planners can will new meanings into being; situated: a symbol's meaning depends on its audience and place.","Middle: habitus is internalised structure that informs but does not determine practice; universal: their eight-step model applies to any city-region.","Middle: habitus is internalised structure that informs but does not determine practice; situated: a symbol's meaning depends on its audience and place.","Structure: habitus determines practice, so symbols merely reproduce existing social positions; situated: a symbol's meaning depends on audience and place."],a:2,e:"Through Bourdieu's habitus and the card-game image, practice is neither a mechanical reaction to structure nor independent of it, a structuration-type middle position; and meaning depends on audience, place and norm holders, so knowledge is situated. The second option is tempting because Box 1 is a general research model, but a general method for studying meaning does not make what it finds universal: each symbol's efficacy is local."},
{t:"dembski",lv:4,fmt:"multi",q:"Dembski & Salet write that 'the logic of appropriateness ranks higher than efficiency'. The HC6 lecture says rational choice actors maximise self-interest that is 'not only economic'. Which statement about the difference between the two is correct?",o:["The difference is only about content: rational choice counts money, while Dembski & Salet count status, identity and other non-economic rewards.","There is no real difference: once self-interest is 'not only economic', rational choice already covers people following norms simply because they are appropriate.","Dembski & Salet reject efficiency as a planning value, so unlike rational choice they recommend symbols even where these waste public resources.","Rational choice admits non-economic interests but takes preferences as given and rules as external; for D&S, institutions shape preferences themselves."],a:3,e:"The split is not about economic versus non-economic motives but about where preferences come from: rational choice treats them as given and institutions as external constraints, whereas Dembski & Salet see internalised institutions shaping basic preferences, so people follow rules from an assumed imperative rather than calculation. The second option is tempting because the lecture does widen self-interest, but a widened calculus is still calculation, not a logic of appropriateness."},
// ===== moroni =====
{t:"moroni",lv:4,fmt:"multi",q:"Moroni and Scott both distrust engineering-style planning. Which statement correctly distinguishes their targets and remedies?",o:["Scott defends property and freedom of contract as evolved institutions; Moroni defends Jane Jacobs's street ethnography against statistical models of slum clearance.","Moroni targets second-order taxis, rules redesigned to an end-state, and prefers general rules; Scott targets high-modernist schemes and values practical knowledge.","Both conclude that planning should be abolished, since any intervention by the state repeats the high-modernist mistake at a higher level.","Moroni's critique rests on the class interests behind state plans, whereas Scott's critique rests on the dispersed knowledge stored in evolved rules."],a:1,e:"Moroni's new target is 'second-order' taxis, the engineering attitude applied to institutions, and his remedy is a kosmos approach of general, open, gradual rules; Scott's target is Le Corbusier-style total plans and Brasília, and his remedy, via Jacobs, is respect for everyday practical knowledge. The first option is tempting because Moroni does cite Jacobs, but only as an invisible-hand explanation, and the defence of property and contract is Moroni's, not Scott's."},
{t:"moroni",lv:4,fmt:"multi",q:"Hayek (1944) argues that central planning presupposes a 'complete ethical code'; Moroni's 'peculiarity' principle says institutions have 'meta-ends'. How do the two connect?",o:["Both say general rules must not serve one substantive goal: Hayek as no agreed ranking of all ends exists, Moroni as institutions serve meta-ends like peaceful coexistence.","Moroni corrects Hayek: once a democratic majority agrees on one social goal, institutions may be redesigned around it, since that goal then becomes their legitimate meta-end.","Both hold that organisations cannot pursue specific ends either, since any organisation that commands its members toward one goal turns into a dictatorship.","Hayek's 'complete ethical code' is what Moroni calls adaptive efficiency: the full ranking of ends toward which general rules must allocate known resources."],a:0,e:"Hayek's point is that planning toward one goal requires agreement on all ends, which nobody has, so collective action should be limited to general rules; Moroni's peculiarity principle says institutions, unlike organisations, have meta-ends and cannot be subordinated to preferred outcomes. The third option fails because Moroni explicitly lets organisations serve specific ends through commands: the distinction is between organisations and institutions."},
{t:"moroni",lv:4,fmt:"assertion",q:"Statement I: Moroni concludes that institutional evolution tends to produce optimal rules, which is why reformers should recognise rather than invent. Statement II: Moroni notes that evolution works on the chance variations that arise rather than on all possible options.",o:["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],a:3,e:"I is false and II is true. Moroni says evolution produces sub-optimal results, precisely because it works on the variations that happen to arise, yet it achieves things no designer could; his case for recognising rather than inventing rests on dispersed knowledge and radical ignorance, not on optimality, so II actually undercuts I."},
{t:"moroni",lv:4,fmt:"mechanism",q:"Which causal chain correctly reconstructs Moroni's route from 'radical ignorance' to reform through 'successor institutions'?",o:["Ignorance in a complex world is only bounded rationality → better models and data can reduce it → reformers can then compute optimal rules → so new rules should replace the old ones in one step.","Ignorance in a complex world cannot be eradicated → no planner can gather the dispersed knowledge evolved rules embody → so existing rules must be frozen → reformers should not touch formal institutions at all.","Ignorance in a complex world cannot be eradicated → no planner can gather the dispersed knowledge evolved rules embody → so reform improves existing rules gradually and indirectly → reformed rules are 'successor institutions'.","Ignorance in a complex world cannot be eradicated → participatory deliberation can pool the dispersed knowledge evolved rules embody → so a new code can be drafted with all stakeholders → reformed rules are 'successor institutions'."],a:2,e:"Moroni's 'radical ignorance' is more than limited rationality: it cannot be overcome, so no mind can grasp the practical knowledge stored in evolved rules; reform must improve rather than reconstruct, gradually and indirectly, never from a tabula rasa. The second chain is tempting but fails at its third link: acknowledging evolution 'does not prevent us from intervening', and Moroni says the issue is not conservatism versus change."},
{t:"moroni",lv:4,fmt:"transfer",q:"Hypothetical: a Dutch province decides that every rezoning will be individually negotiated, with contributions set case by case and the rules revised each year. Which pairing of a Moroni analysis with a historical institutionalist critique is correct?",o:["Moroni: case-by-case flexibility is a kosmos approach, since it adapts rules to each local situation; HI critique: such rules are distributive and contested, products of earlier junctures, not neutral evolved orders.","Moroni: case-specific, yearly revised rules violate openness, knowability and robustness; HI critique: such rules are distributive and contested, products of earlier junctures, not neutral evolved orders.","Moroni: case-specific, yearly revised rules violate openness, knowability and robustness; HI critique: rules only work if they make sense to actors and cannot get their meaning by administrative fiat.","Moroni: case-specific, yearly revised rules violate openness, knowability and robustness; HI critique: insiders will rent-seek in each negotiation, so value is shifted onto future homebuyers."],a:1,e:"Moroni wants general rules for an indeterminate number of future cases (openness), known in advance (knowability) and stable (robustness); yearly, case-specific bargaining violates all three. A historical institutionalist like Sorensen would answer that such rules, for instance Toronto's Section 37, are power-laden and contested along a pathway, not a benign selection of efficient rules; the third critique is Moroni's own sense-making principle and the fourth is Taylor's rent-seeking."},
{t:"moroni",lv:4,fmt:"dimension",q:"Which placement of Moroni (2010) on deliberate vs emergent change AND universal vs situated knowledge, with its justification, matches the course reading?",o:["Emergent: most institutions are the unintended outcome of slow evolution; situated: the knowledge they embody is dispersed and practical, beyond any single planner.","Emergent: most institutions are the unintended outcome of slow evolution; universal: his openness principle calls for universal rules that cover all future cases.","Deliberate: reform is possible through ten principles applied by planners; situated: the knowledge they embody is dispersed and practical, beyond any single planner.","Deliberate: institutions are artificial, so they are made by human decision; universal: his openness principle calls for universal rules that cover all future cases."],a:0,e:"Moroni stresses emergence (institutions as 'the result of human action, but not the execution of any human design') and an epistemology of dispersed, tacit, practical knowledge, which argues against universal blueprints. The second option is the trap: 'universal rules' in the openness principle describes the scope of rules (general, not case-specific), not a claim that anyone holds universal knowledge."},
// ===== sorensen =====
{t:"sorensen",lv:4,fmt:"multi",q:"Bouwmeester's Swiss hypothesis (HC6) and Sorensen's Toronto study both help explain why places differ in how they densify or capture land value. Which statement correctly contrasts them?",o:["Both attribute the differences to path-dependent lock-in, so neither expects the rules on who gains from land value to change once set.","Sorensen explains Toronto by rational landowners' strategies; Bouwmeester explains Switzerland by critical junctures with permissive and productive conditions.","Bouwmeester derives a testable expectation from incentives a property rule creates; Sorensen traces variation to contingent histories of junctures.","Both are deliberately situated case studies that refuse to formulate any hypothesis that could be tested in other places or countries."],a:2,e:"The Swiss case is a rational choice hypothesis: the BGBB shapes landowners' incentives so densification becomes their rational strategy, presented in the lecture as a testable expectation; Sorensen explains Toronto's 10 to 15 percent versus Vancouver's 70 percent through contingent institutional histories. The second option simply swaps the two theories, which is why it can look right at a glance."},
{t:"sorensen",lv:4,fmt:"assertion",q:"Statement I: For Sorensen, a housing crisis does not by itself determine whether a critical juncture strengthens or weakens land value capture. Statement II: In Sorensen's model, permissive conditions open institutions to change, while productive conditions such as actors, ideology and timing shape the specific outcome.",o:["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],a:0,e:"Both are true, and II explains I: a housing crisis is a permissive condition; all three Toronto junctures followed one, yet two strengthened value capture and the latest weakened it, because 'differing productive conditions determined the outcome'. Treating the statements as unrelated misses that the permissive/productive split is exactly Sorensen's explanation of this pattern."},
{t:"sorensen",lv:4,fmt:"mechanism",q:"Which causal chain correctly reconstructs Sorensen's claim that path dependence is usually 'not a matter of lock-in'?",o:["A critical juncture sets rules and their ambiguities → beneficiaries and losers fight over them → the winners' rules then become locked in → so contestation ends until the next external shock.","A critical juncture sets rules and their ambiguities → beneficiaries defend and losers contest them in a strategic action field → contestation brings constant incremental change → but within a constrained developmental pathway.","A critical juncture sets rules and their ambiguities → beneficiaries defend and losers contest them in a strategic action field → each contest opens a new critical juncture of its own → so the pathway is redrawn every few years.","A critical juncture sets rules and their ambiguities → every actor internalises the new rules as appropriate → nobody contests them any more → so the pathway then changes only through slow, gradual cultural drift."],a:1,e:"Sorensen: 'contestation is continuous and incremental change constant, but within a developmental pathway representing a relatively constrained range of options'; the juncture sets the terrain and the rules of the field. The third chain is tempting but fails at its third link: Sorensen separates junctures from the ongoing reactive sequence, and even treats Toronto's post-1997 expansion of development charges as change of practice within existing rules."},
{t:"sorensen",lv:4,fmt:"transfer",q:"Hypothetical: after a major flood, a Dutch province gains new powers and passes a law making developers in rebuilt areas fund dike works; the law is vague on whether existing owners pay too, and a decade of court cases follows. Which pairing of a Sorensen analysis with a rational choice critique is correct?",o:["Sorensen: the flood is the productive condition that fixed the law's content; RC critique: the analysis should show who gains from each clause and how concentrated interests lobby for it.","Sorensen: the flood opened a juncture, the coalition shaped the law, its ambiguity began a reactive sequence; RC critique: the law failed because it did not fit internalised norms of fairness.","Sorensen: the flood opened a juncture, the coalition shaped the law, its ambiguity began a reactive sequence; RC critique: such rules should evolve gradually, not be imposed after a single shock.","Sorensen: the flood opened a juncture, the coalition shaped the law, its ambiguity began a reactive sequence; RC critique: show who gains from each clause and how concentrated interests lobby."],a:3,e:"A disaster is a classic permissive condition; the outcome is shaped by productive conditions (the coalition, legal structures), and an ambiguous law triggers a reactive sequence, as Ontario's 1946 Act did. A rational choice critic would push the analysis down to actors' incentives and rent-seeking; the first option misplaces the flood as the productive condition, the second critique is sociological and the third evolutionary."},
{t:"sorensen",lv:4,fmt:"dimension",q:"Which placement of Sorensen (2024) on structure vs agency AND reproduction vs transformation, with its justification, matches the course reading?",o:["Structure-leaning with agency at junctures; both: bounded pathways reproduce rules, while junctures and incremental change transform them.","Structure only, with no role for agency; reproduction: path dependence has locked in the value capture rules first set in 1946.","Agency-dominant: every outcome follows from governing parties' free choices; transformation only, since each new government rewrites the rules.","Structure-leaning with agency at junctures; reproduction only, since even the 2018 to 2023 Bills kept development charges and density bonuses."],a:0,e:"Structure dominates most of the time (the developmental pathway), but agency matters strongly in junctures, when choices are contingent; Sorensen explains both reproduction (bounded pathways) and transformation (junctures such as 1989, plus incremental change). The last option is tempting because Sorensen notes that the basic tools survived recent cuts, but he also treats those cuts as a likely third juncture that sharply reduced value capture."},
{t:"sorensen",lv:4,fmt:"multi",q:"Needham writes that property rights 'can be deliberately changed in order purposefully to influence land use'. Sorensen traces how Ontario actually changed its value capture rules. Which statement combines the two correctly?",o:["Sorensen refutes Needham: path dependence means such rules can no longer be deliberately changed once a pathway has been set.","Both describe rule change as slow, unintended evolution in which no single actor's deliberate choice decides the outcome.","Needham himself shows that rights change mainly at critical junctures, which Sorensen then confirms with the Toronto history of value capture.","Sorensen qualifies Needham: rules can be changed on purpose, but big change clusters in crisis-opened junctures, then stays contested."],a:3,e:"Needham establishes that rules are a policy variable; Sorensen shows when and how such deliberate change happens: in critical junctures opened by crises, shaped by productive conditions, followed by reactive sequences of contestation. The first option is tempting, but Sorensen explicitly rejects lock-in and documents deliberate legislative change in 1946, 1989 and after 2018."}
];
